<h1>George Cardenas</h1>
<p>Candidate for Mayor, Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027</p>
<p><a href="https://cardenas4chicago.com" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website</a></p>
<p><a href="https://cardenas4chicago.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">George Cardenas&#39;s campaign publishes nine pillars, each laid out as a problem, a set of commitments, and a 100-day plan. Tap here to read them on his site, then come back to comment.</a></p>
<h2>AI summary</h2>
<h3>The platform</h3>
<p>Police: Add 200 officers, 50 detectives, and 75 transit police, plus $15 million a year for violence intervention. The goal is 70% of residents feeling safe by 2030.</p>
<p>Housing: Allow four-flats, courtyard buildings, and more homes near transit. Take long-abandoned property through eminent domain and put it back to use.</p>
<p>Money: A new Auditor General to track waste, contracting changes Cardenas says save $750 million a year, and fully funded pensions in 30 years.</p>
<p>Families: $3,000 yearly stipends for working families, universal pre-K, and 12 new community health centers. Every issue comes with a 100-day plan.</p>
<h3>Next to the other candidates</h3>
<p>Only Cardenas: A new Auditor General to track waste and contracts.</p>
<p>Cash to households: Carlisle backs guaranteed income, Cardenas proposes $3,000 yearly stipends for working families, and Brooks proposes a $3,500 credit for family caregivers.</p>
<p>Vacant land: Cardenas would take long-abandoned property by eminent domain. Carlisle would tax idle land more heavily, and Quigley would build on city-owned lots.</p>
<p>Not covered: K-12 schools beyond pre-K and tutoring. Holberg, Kelly, Wilson, Brewer, Carlisle, and Su address them.</p>
